-- An imported rule that reads a section GIVEN, in every position an operand can
-- take (smucclaw/l4-ide#1115).
--
-- `the window` and the rest come from ok/section-given-import-operand-def.l4,
-- where `rate` is 3 by default, so `the window` is 30. Named here, each is a
-- closure that has to be applied to that module's own cell before it is a
-- value. Evaluating `the window` on its own always did that. Used as an
-- operand it did not: arithmetic and comparison failed with an internal error,
-- equality said one side was a function, and a list or a record that held it
-- held the function. In a single file, with the section GIVEN beside the rule,
-- every line below gives the value written next to it.
--
-- Supplying `rate` from here is not possible, as in section-given-import-call.l4:
-- the imported file's own default is what applies. A rule that reads a section
-- GIVEN with no default is in ok/section-given-import-operand-unset.l4.

IMPORT `section-given-import-operand-def`

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`bump` x MEANS x PLUS 1

GIVETH A NUMBER
`mine` MEANS `the window`

GIVETH A NUMBER
`window plus one` MEANS `the window` PLUS 1

GIVEN f IS A FUNCTION FROM NUMBER AND NUMBER TO Pair
      p IS A NUMBER
      q IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A Pair
`build` f p q MEANS f p q

GIVEN g IS A FUNCTION FROM NUMBER TO NUMBER
      v IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`apply to` g v MEANS g v

GIVEN h IS A FUNCTION FROM Pair TO NUMBER
      p IS A Pair
GIVETH A NUMBER
`read with` h p MEANS h p

-- On its own, and wrapped in a rule of this file. Both worked before.
#EVAL `the window`                                  -- 30
#EVAL `mine` PLUS 1                                 -- 31

-- The operand inside the body of a rule of this file, which is where it is
-- usually written.
#EVAL `window plus one`                             -- 31

-- As an operand of arithmetic.
#EVAL `the window` PLUS 1                           -- 31
#EVAL 1 PLUS `the window`                           -- 31
#EVAL `the window` MINUS 1                          -- 29
#EVAL `the window` TIMES 2                          -- 60
#EVAL `the window` DIVIDED BY 2                     -- 15

-- As an operand of a comparison, and of equality.
#EVAL `the window` > 0                              -- TRUE
#EVAL `the window` AT LEAST 31                      -- FALSE
#EVAL `the window` EQUALS 30                        -- TRUE
#EVAL `is big` EQUALS TRUE                          -- TRUE
#ASSERT `the window` EQUALS 30
#ASSERT (`the window` PLUS 1) EQUALS 31

-- With the connectives and IF, which worked before.
#EVAL `is big` AND TRUE                             -- TRUE
#EVAL NOT `is big`                                  -- FALSE
#EVAL IF `is big` THEN 1 ELSE 2                     -- 1

-- Held in a list or a record, and passed to a rule of this file.
#EVAL LIST `the window`, 1                          -- LIST 30, 1
#EVAL Pair `the window` 2                           -- Pair OF 30, 2
#EVAL (Pair `the window` 2)'s a PLUS 1              -- 31
#EVAL `bump` `the window`                           -- 31

-- Projected from, and matched on.
#EVAL `the pair`'s a                                -- 3
#EVAL (`the pair`'s a) PLUS 1                       -- 4
#EVAL CONSIDER `the window`
  WHEN 30 THEN "thirty"
  OTHERWISE "other"                                 -- "thirty"

-- A rule with an input of its own, applied: worked before, and the control.
-- So are such a rule and a record constructor passed on as values: they are
-- functions, and must stay functions.
#EVAL `scaled` 2                                    -- 6
#EVAL `scaled` 2 PLUS 1                             -- 7
#EVAL `apply to` `scaled` 2                         -- 6
#EVAL `build` Pair 1 2                              -- Pair OF 1, 2
#EVAL `read with` a (Pair 7 8)                      -- 7

-- A rule that returns a function, passed on as one.
#EVAL `apply to` `adder` 5                          -- 8
